So consider  $M,N,P,Q$  to be midpoints of sides  $CD,AD,AB,BC$  respectively  and   $O$  to be the center of square. now  $MNPQ$  also forms a square so we get: $f(A)+f(B)+f(C)+f(D)=0$  and  $f(M)+f(N)+f(P)+f(Q)=0$ now also we have that  $MOQC , OPBQ, OPAN, OMDN$  to be also square so we get that: $f(N)+f(P)=f(C)+f(O)$  $f(Q)+f(P)=f(D)+f(O)$  $f(Q)+f(M)=f(A)+f(O)$  $f(M)+f(N)=f(B)+f(O)$ which gives us  $2(f(M)+f(N)+f(P)+f(Q))=f(A)+f(B)+f(C)+f(D)+4f(O)$ which gives  $4f(O)=0\implies f(O)=0$  , now we can shift this point any where in the place and hence we get that  $f(P)=0$   $\forall$  points  $P$  in the plane.  $\blacksquare$

\begin{align*}
4f(P) &= \sum_{a=0}^3 (f(P)+f(P+i^a)+f(P+i^{a+1})+f(P+i^a+i^{a+1}))- \sum_{a=0}^3 f(P+i^a+i^{a+1}) - 2 \sum_{a=0}^3 f(P+i^a) 
&= 4\cdot 0-0-2\cdot 0 
&= 0
\end{align*}

That's the form that Kiran Kedlaya *et al.* used for the answer in the solutions he posted on the AMC website. To be specific, he said
\[ f(x) \equal{} 2^{\minus{}1/12}
