Given two integers  $n\geq 1$  and  $q\geq 2$ , let  $A=\{(a_1,\ldots ,a_n):a_i\in\{0,\ldots ,q-1\}, i=1,\ldots ,n\}$ . If  $a=(a_1,\ldots ,a_n)$  and  $b=(b_1,\ldots ,b_n)$  are two elements of  $A$ , let  $\delta(a,b)=\#\{i:a_i\neq b_i\}$ . Let further  $t$  be a non-negative integer and  $B$  a non-empty subset of  $A$  such that  $\delta(a,b)\geq 2t+1$ , whenever  $a$  and  $b$  are distinct elements of  $B$ . Prove that the two statements below are equivalent:
a) For any  $a\in A$ , there is a unique  $b\in B$ , such that  $\delta (a,b)\leq t$ ;
b)  $\displaystyle|B|\cdot \sum_{k=0}^t \binom{n}{k}(q-1)^k=q^n$

candidates:
We denote a permutation satisfying the condition as a good-permutation. Let  $b_n$  be the number of good-permutations of  $\{1,\ldots,n\}$ , we claim that  $b_1=1,b_2=2, b_n=2^{n-2}\cdot 3$  for all  $n \geq 3$ .

It is easy to verify that  $b_1=1, b_2=2$  so we will prove the case for  $n \geq 3$ . We will first prove the following claim. 
Claim: For any  $k \geq 4$  and consecutive integers  $\{a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_k\}$  ordered increasingly, it can only remain a good-permutation if we remove  $a_1$  or  $a_k$ .

Proof: Since we have  $k$  consecutive integers,  $a_1,\ldots,a_{k-1}$  must form a complete residue system  $\mod{k-1}$  and  $a_k \equiv a_1 \mod{k-1}$ . Suppose we removed  $a_i$ , then we must have that 
\begin{align*}
2(\frac{(k-1)k}{2}+a_k-a_i) &\equiv 0 \mod{k-1}
(k-1)k+2(a_k-a_i) &\equiv 0 \mod{k-1}
2a_k &\equiv 2a_i \mod{k-1}
\end{align*}
if  $k-1$  is odd then  $a_k \equiv a_i \mod{k-1} \implies a_k \equiv a_k-(k-i) \mod{k-1} \implies i \equiv 1 \mod{k-1}$ . Since  $1 \leq i \leq k$ , we must have that  $i=1, k$  and we are done. Suppose now that  $k-1$  is even and that  $1<i<k$ , then   $$ 2a_k \equiv 2(a_k-(k-i)) \mod{k-1} \implies 2k \equiv 2i \mod{k-1} \implies 2i \equiv 2 \mod{k-1} $$   
Thus we can write  $2i=(k-1)t+2$ , if  $t \geq 2$  then we have  $2i \geq 2k \implies i \geq k$  and if  $t=0$  then  $i=1$ , so  $t=1$  and  $2i=k+1, i=\frac{k+1}{2}$ . Now, suppose we have removed that number. Then we are left with   $$ a_1, \ldots, a_{i-1}, a_{i+1}, \ldots, a_k $$  Note that  $a_1,\ldots,a_i, \ldots, a_{k-2}$  originally formed a complete residue system  $\mod{k-2}$ , we must have that  $a_{k-1} \equiv a_2, a_k \equiv a_1 \mod{k-2}$ . Since this is a good-permutation, we must be able to remove another  $a_j$  such that the resulting numbers is still able to be made a good-permutation. That is, there exists some  $a_j$  such that  $j \neq i$  where 
\begin{align*}
2(\frac{(k-2)(k-1)}{2}+a_{k-1}+a_k-a_i-a_j) &\equiv 0 \mod{k-2}
2(a_{k-1}+a_k) &\equiv 2(a_i+a_j) \mod{k-2}
\end{align*}
Since  $k-1$  is even,  $k-2$  must be odd, so this is equivalent to 
\begin{align*}
a_{k-1} + a_k &\equiv a_i+a_j \mod{k-2}
2a_k-1 &\equiv a_k-(k-i)+a_k-(k-j) \mod{k-2}
2a_k-1 &\equiv 2a_k-2k+i+j \mod{k-2}
2k-1 &\equiv i+j \mod{k-2}
i+j &\equiv 3 \mod{k-2}
\end{align*}
So  $i+j=(k-2)t+3$ , if  $t \geq 3$  then  $i+j \geq 3k-3 > 2k$  since  $k>3$ . Thus we have that  $i+j=3, k+1, 2k-1$ . If  $i+j=3$  then  $j=3-\frac{k+1}{2}=\frac{5-k}{2}$ , but since  $k \geq 4$ , this will always be less than  $1$ . If  $i+j=2k-1$  then  $j=2k-1-\frac{k+1}{2}=\frac{4k-2-k-1}{2}=\frac{3(k-1)}{2}$ , but  $\frac{3(k-1)}{2} > k \implies 3k-3>2k \implies k>3$  which is true so this can't be possible either. If  $i+j=k+1$  then  $j=\frac{k+1}{2}=i$ , but we already removed  $a_i$  from the set of numbers so we cannot do it again. Thus  $i=1,k$ , it is easy to verify both of those values work.

Now with this claim we know that for every  $\{1,\ldots,n\}$ , the  $n$ th place of the permutation must be  $1,n$ , then it passes a set of  $n-1$  consecutive integers down and the  $n-1$ st place of the permutation must be the min or the max as well. By induction this continues until we get to the  $3$ rd place of the permutation. Here,  $1,2$  clearly will always satisfy the divisbility criteria no matter what the first two numbers of the permutation are, and for any three consecutive integers the divisibility criteria for  $k=3$  is also always satisfied, so we can arrange the first three numbers however we want. Thus there are  $6$  ways to determine the first three places and then at each place after that we get  $2$  choices, so for any  $n \geq 3$ , there are  $2^{n-3} \cdot 6 =2^{n-2} \cdot 3$  good-permutations.

