We can also compute as follows. Notation as in Namdung's answer. 

Let  $f(E) = E^2-2E-1$ . We have  $f(E)a_n = 0$  and  $f(E)b_n = 0$  and
\begin{align*}
f(E)(c_n - c_{n-2}) = f(E)(b_{n-1} + b_{n-2}) = 0
\end{align*}
Hence
\begin{align*}
f(E)c_n = f(E)c_{n-2}
\end{align*}
When  $n=2m$  is even, we have 
\begin{align*}
f(E)c_{2m} &= f(E)c_{2m-2} = \cdots = f(E)c_0 
&=c_2 - 2c_1 - c_0 
&= 4 - 2 \cdot 1 - 1 
&=1
\end{align*}
When  $n=2m+1$  is odd, we have 
\begin{align*}
f(E)c_{2m+1} &= f(E)c_{2m-1} = \cdots = f(E)c_1 
&=c_3 - 2c_2 - c_1 
&= 8 - 2 \cdot 4 - 1 
&=-1
\end{align*}
Hence we can write  $f(E)c_n = (-1)^n$ . We now have
\begin{align*}
f(E)S_n = f(E)a_n - f(E)c_{n-2} = (-1)^{n+1}
\end{align*}
Thus  $S_n$  satisfies the recurrence relation
\begin{align*}
S_{n+2} - 2S_{n+1} - S_n = (-1)^{n+1}
\end{align*}
This is a non homogeneous recurrence relation with characteristic roots  $1 \pm \sqrt{2}$  and the general solution can be written as 
\begin{align*}
S_n = A(1+\sqrt{2})^n + B(1-\sqrt{2})^n + C(-1)^n
\end{align*}
 Using  $S_1 = 3, S_2 = 6$  and  $S_3 = 16$ , we can obtain 
 \begin{align*}
 A = \dfrac{3+\sqrt{2}}{4}, \, B = \dfrac{3-\sqrt{2}}{4}, \, C = -\dfrac{1}{2}
 \end{align*}
 and we obtain the same expression for  $S_n$  as before.

