<details><summary>Solution</summary>It suffices to take mod 4 and mod 25 - first, trivially since there are more multiples of 2 than 5,  $90! \equiv 0 \pmod 4$ .

Next we take mod 25. The key observation is that **<span style="color:#f00">Claim.**</span>   $$ (5n+1)(5n+2)(5n+3)(5n+4) = 24 \equiv -1 \pmod {25}. $$  *Proof.* Expanding and analyzing the  $n$  and constant terms suffices.

Now we rewrite:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4 \cdot 5 \cdots 90}{10^{\nu_{10}(90!)}} 
    &= (1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4)\cdot (6 \cdot 7 \cdot 8 \cdot 9)\cdots(86 \cdot 87 \cdot 88 \cdot 89) \cdot \frac{5 \cdot 10 \cdot 15 \cdots 90}{5^{\nu_5(90!)}2^{\nu_5(90!)}} 
    &\equiv (-1)^{18} \cdot \frac{1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3 \cdots 18}{5^{\nu_5(90!)}2^{\nu_5(90!)}} \pmod {25} 
    &\equiv \frac{18!}{5^{21}2^{21}} \pmod {25}
    &\equiv \frac{18!}{5^{21} 2^{2\phi(25)+1}} \pmod {25} 
    &\equiv \frac{18!}{5^{21} \cdot 2} \pmod {25} 
    &\equiv (1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3) \cdot (1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4) \cdots (11 \cdot 12 \cdot 13 \cdot 14) \cdot 16 \cdot 17 \cdot 18 \pmod {25} \cdot \frac 12 
    &\equiv-( 3 \cdot 16 \cdot 17 \cdot 18) \pmod {25} 
    &\equiv -(3 \cdot (-9) \cdot (-8) \cdot (-7)) \pmod {25} 
    &\equiv 27 \cdot 56 \pmod {25} 
    &\equiv 12 \pmod {25}
\end{align*}
and combining using CRT or equivalent methods gives the answer of  $\boxed{12} \implies \textbf{(A)}$ .</details>

