solution_md:
You don't need angular acceleration to find angular momentum.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>[asy]
draw(circle((0,0),5));
dot((0,0));
dot((0,5),red);
label("Harry",(0,5),N);
draw((0,0)--(0,5),dashed);
label(" $r$ ",(0,3),E);
[/asy]

The only force acting on poor Harry Potter is the centripetal force. The centripetal acceleration is  $a_R=\omega^2r$ . He passes out when it is 5.0 g or  $50 \ m/s^2$ .  $$ \omega^2r=50 \ m/s^2 $$    $$ \omega^2(2.0 \ m)=50 \ m/s^2 $$    $$ \omega=5.0 \ rad/s $$  The angular momentum  $L$  is  $I\omega$ . This can be rewritten as:  $$ L=I\omega=mr^2\omega $$  .

We substitute our variables in and get:  $$ L=(50.0 \ kg)(2.0 \ m)^2(5.0 \ rad/s)=1000 \ kg \cdot m^2/s $$  Thus, the answer is D.</details>

