solution_md:
Tension is uniform throughout the string, so our first goal must be to find the tension in the string. Finding the tension is easiest in the first endpoint. By creating a freebody diagram we have
\[\sum{F}=0=T-mg\implies T=mg\]
We note that the center of mass of the system has to be a distance of  $a/2$  from the first endpoint because the two endpoints are separated by a distance  $a$ . Therefore the middle mass must be in the center of the system. 

Since the middle block is a distance  $a/2$  then the angle the string makes with the horizontal is  $30^{\circ}$  from simple geometry of a freebody diagram. 
[asy]
dot((0,0));
draw((0,0)--(sqrt(3)/2, 1/2), arrow=Arrow(4));
draw((0,0)--(-sqrt(3)/2, 1/2), arrow=Arrow(4));
draw((0,0)--(0, -sqrt(3)/2), arrow=Arrow(4));
label(" $mg$ ", (sqrt(3)/2, 1/2), NE);
label(" $mg$ ", (-sqrt(3)/2, 1/2), NW);
label(" $mg$ ", (0, -sqrt(3)/2), S);
[/asy]
Therefore 
\[\tan{30^{\circ}}=\frac{b}{a/2}=\frac{2b}{a}\]
\[\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}=\frac{2b}{a}\]
\[\frac{a}{b}=2\sqrt{3}\]
Thus the answer is  $\boxed{E}$ .

