solution_md:
The previous solutions don't really explain the simplification process, so below is a more detailed solution. We will be using identities from the document in **[this thread](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13188h1208277_useful_identities_and_inequalities_in_algebra)**.

Let  $p=x-y$  and  $q=y-z$ . Then  $z-x=-(p+q)$ . This gives us
\begin{align*}
&\, \frac{(x - y)^7 + (y - z)^7 + (z - x)^7 - (x - y)(y - z)(z - x) \left[(x - y)^4 + (y - z)^4 + (z - x)^4\right]}
{(x - y)^5 + (y - z)^5 + (z - x)^5}
=&\, \frac{p^7 + q^7 - (p+q)^7 + pq(p+q)\left[p^4 + q^4 + (p+q)^4\right]}{p^5 + q^5 - (p+q)^5}
=&\, \frac{\left[(p+q)^7 - p^7 - q^7\right]-pq(p+q)\left[p^4 + q^4 + (p+q)^4\right]}{(p+q)^5 - p^5 - q^5}.
\end{align*}
Now we use the factorization identities
\begin{align*}
(p+q)^7 - p^7 - q^7 &= 7pq(p+q)(p^2 + pq + q^2)^2
p^4 + q^4 + (p+q)^4 &= 2(p^2 + pq + q^2)^2
(p+q)^5 - p^5 - q^5 &= 5pq(p+q)(p^2 + pq + q^2).
\end{align*}
This lets us transform the expression so far into
\begin{align*}
\frac{\left[(p+q)^7 - p^7 - q^7\right]-pq(p+q)\left[p^4 + q^4 + (p+q)^4\right]}{(p+q)^5 - p^5 - q^5}
&= \frac{7pq(p+q)(p^2 + pq + q^2)^2-2pq(p+q)(p^2 + pq + q^2)^2}{5pq(p+q)(p^2 + pq + q^2)}
&= \frac{5pq(p+q)(p^2 + pq + q^2)^2}{5pq(p+q)(p^2 + pq + q^2)}
&= p^2 + pq + q^2.
\end{align*}
Now we go back to  $x,y,z$  to get
\begin{align*}
p^2 + pq + q^2 &= (x-y)^2 + (x-y)(y-z) + (y-z)^2
&= x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - xy - yz - zx
&= \frac{1}{2}\left[(x-y)^2+(y-z)^2+(z-x)^2\right].
\end{align*}
So we are trying to show that  $(x-y)^2+(y-z)^2+(z-x)^2\ge 3\cdot 2 = 6$  for distinct integers. The left is symmetric in  $x,y,z$  pairwise, so we can assume without loss of generality that  $x>y>z$ . Then  $p=x-y\ge 1$  and  $q = y-z\ge 1$  and we can rewrite
\begin{align*}
(x-y)^2+(y-z)^2+(z-x)^2 &= p^2 + q^2 + (p+q)^2
&\ge 1^2 + 1^2 + (1+1)^2
&= 6,
\end{align*} as desired. Equality holds if and only if  $x,y,z$  form consecutive integers in some order.

