solution_md:
We have
\begin{align*}
 \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} f(n) &=  \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k^n \cdot k!} &=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}\left(\sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k^n}\right)\frac{1}{k!} &=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}\left(\frac{1/k^2}{1-1/k}\right)\frac{1}{k!} &=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{k(k-1)k!} & = \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \left(\frac{1}{k-1}-\frac{1}{k}\right) \frac{1}{k!}&= \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{(k-1) \cdot k!}-\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k \cdot k!}.  \end{align*}

Using the familiar power series  $e^x =\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{x^k}{k!}$ , we see that  $\frac{e^x -x-1}{x}=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{x ^{k-1}}{k!}$   and   $\int_{0}^{1} \frac{e^x -x-1}{x} dx=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k \cdot k!}.$ Similarly, we see that   $\frac {e^x -x -1}{x^2}=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac {x^ {k-2}}{k!}$  and  $\int_{0}^{1} \frac {e^x -x -1}{x^2}dx=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{(k-1) \cdot k!}.$   
Therefore, we conclude that
\begin{align*}  \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} f(n) &= \sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{(k-1) \cdot k!}-\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k \cdot k!} &=\int_{0}^{1} \frac {e^x -x -1}{x^2}dx - \int_{0}^{1} \frac{e^x -x-1}{x} dx =\int_{0}^{1} \frac{e^x -xe^x+x^2-1}{x^2}dx=\boxed{3-e}.      \end{align*}

Finally,  $3-e$  is approximately  $ 0.281718172 $ .

