By Power of a Point on  $\Gamma$ , we have that  $SC^2=SB \times SA$ . Since  $SC=SP$ , we have  $SP^2=SB \times SA$ . By the converse of Power of a Point on ( $APB$ ), this implies  $SP$  is tangent to ( $APB$ ). Therefore, by tangent criterion, we have  $\angle{SPB}=\frac{(BP)}{2}$ . Let ( $AB$ ) denote the major arc  $AB$  in ( $APB$ ). By Inscribed Angle Theorem, we have  $\angle{APB}=\frac{(AB)}{2}$ . Note: Here ( $APB$ ) denotes the circumcircle and not arc measure.

We now prove the following lemma:

Lemma:  $\angle{KPS}=\angle{PBA}$ .
Proof: We have  $\angle{KPS}=180-\angle{SPB}-\angle{APB}=180-\frac{(BP)}{2}-\frac{(AB)}{2}=180-\frac{1}{2}((BP)+(AB))=180-\frac{1}{2}(360-(AP))=\frac{1}{2}(AP)=\angle{PBA}$  as desired. 

We are now ready to finish. All angle measures from now on are taken from  $\Gamma$ . Note that  $(ML)=(MA)+(AL)$ .

We have  $\angle{KPC}=\frac{(KC)+(MA)}{2}$  so  $(MA)=2\angle{KPC}-(KC)$ . Therefore, we have  $(ML)=(MA)+(AL)=2\angle{KPC}-(KC)+(AL)=2\angle{KPC}-(KC)+2\angle{ABL}=2(\angle{KPC}+\angle{ABL})-(KC)=2(\angle{KPC}+\angle{KPS})-(KC)=2\angle{SPC}-(KC)$ .

Since  $SC=SP$ , it is true that  $\angle{SPC}=\angle{SCP}$ . We then have  $(ML)=2\angle{SCP}-(KC)=2\times\frac{(MKC)}{2}-(KC)=(MKC)-(KC)=(MK)$ . This means  $(ML)=(MK)$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$

