*Proof.* We proceed with strong induction, assume that \begin{align*} \operatorname{max} \{ \tfrac{a_i}{i}  \mid 1 \le i \le n-1 \} = \tfrac{a_{\ell}}{\ell}\end{align*}
We have that  $a_n = a_{n-k} + a_k$  for some  $1 \le k \le n-1$ . Therefore, 

\begin{align*} \frac{a_n}{n} = \frac{(n-k) \cdot \tfrac{a_{n-k}}{n-k} + k \cdot \tfrac{a_{k}}{k}}{n}\end{align*} which is essentially a weighted average of  $\tfrac{a_{n-k}}{n-k}$  and  $\tfrac{a_k}{k}$ , implying  $\tfrac{a_n}{n} < \operatorname{max} \left( \tfrac{a_k}{k}, \tfrac{a_{n-k}}{n-k} \right) \le \tfrac{a_\ell}{\ell}.$   $\square$ Define  $b_n \coloneq na_\ell - \ell a_n$ . We now shift our focus from  $(a_i)$  to  $(b_i)$ . First, we express  $(a_i)$  and  $(b_i)$  with discrete integers, rather real numbers with the following claim. **<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>** We may select nonnegative integers  $(\epsilon_1, \epsilon_2, \dots, \epsilon_s)$  such that \begin{align*} b_n = \epsilon_1 b_1 + \epsilon_2 b_2 + \cdots + \epsilon_s b_s\end{align*}

*Proof.* Note by the definition of  $b_n$ , it suffices to prove the analagous claim for  $a_n$ , and this easily follows from employing strong induction.  $\square$ <span style="color:#f00">**Claim:**</span> We have  $b_{n+\ell} \le b_n$  for sufficiently large  $n$ . 

*Proof.* We have \begin{align*} b_{n+\ell} = (n + \ell) a_{\ell} - \ell a_{n+\ell} \le (n+\ell)a_{\ell} - \ell(a_n+a_\ell)  = na_\ell - \ell a_n = b_{n}\end{align*} where equality occurs when  $a_{n+\ell} = a_n + a_\ell$ .  $\square$ To finish, note that, by the second claim about discreteness, the sequence  $\{b_n, \: b_{n + \ell}, \: b_{n+2\ell}, \cdots \}$  must take upon finite values, and therefore must be constant due to how it is non-increasing. Simple rearrangement gives  $a_{n+\ell} = a_n + a_\ell$  for all sufficiently large  $n$ , so we are done.

