Let us use directed angles modulo  $180^{\circ}$  to avoid configuration issues. Since  $\measuredangle AFC=\measuredangle ADC=90^{\circ}$ , we note that the quadrilateral  $AFDC$  is cyclic, so that
\begin{align*}
\measuredangle QFA&=\measuredangle DFA
&=\measuredangle DCA
&=\measuredangle BCA.
\end{align*}
The Inscribed Angle Theorem implies that
\begin{align*}
\measuredangle BCA&=\measuredangle BPA
&=\measuredangle QPA,
\end{align*}
so combining the above two chain of equalities gives   $$ \measuredangle QFA=\measuredangle QPA, $$   implying that quadrilateral  $AFQP$  is cyclic.

Now observe that  $\measuredangle BFC=\measuredangle BEC=90^{\circ}$ , so that quadrilateral  $BFEC$  is cyclic, so that
\begin{align*}
\measuredangle AFE&=\measuredangle BFE
&=\measuredangle BCE
&=\measuredangle BCA
&=\measuredangle QPA,
\end{align*}
where the last step follows from the previous derivation that  $\measuredangle BCA=\measuredangle QPA$  due to the Inscribed Angle Theorem. Since  $AFQP$  is cyclic, combined with the above equality, we conclude that
\begin{align*}
\measuredangle AQP&=\measuredangle AFP
&=\measuredangle AFE
&=\measuredangle QPA,
\end{align*}
so it follows that  $\triangle APQ$  is isoceles with  $AP=AQ$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$

We complex bash on  $(\triangle ABC)$  as the unit circle, setting  $A=x, B=y, C=z$ Let  $I,J$  be the intersections of line  $EF$  with the circumcircle
Let  $G,H$  be the intersections of line  $DF$  with the circumcircle
\begin{align*}
I+J&=E+IJ\overline{E}
&=F+IJ\overline{F}
\end{align*}
Thus   $$ IJ=\frac{E-F}{\overline{F}-\overline{E}}=\frac{\frac{xy}{z}-\frac{xz}{y}}{\frac{y}{xz}-\frac{z}{xy}}=x^2 $$    $$ I+J=\frac{1}{2}(2x+y+z-\frac{xz}{y}+\frac{x^2}{y}+x^2z-\frac{xy}{z}) $$  Denote  $Q'$  on line  $BP$  such that  $AQ'=AP$ . It suffices to show that  $Q'$  lies on lines  $DF$  
Let  $T$  be the foot of perpendicular from  $A$  to  $BP$  $T=1/2(A+P+B-\overline{A}PB)$  $\boxed{Q'=2T-P=A+B-\frac{BP}{A}}$ Similarly to how  $IJ, I+J$  are found,   $$ GH=y^2 $$  ,   $$ G+H=1/2(\frac{y^2}{x}+\frac{y^2}{z}+2y+x+z-\frac{yx}{z}-\frac{yz}{x}) $$  It suffices that  $\overline{Q'DF}$  is collinear  $\iff \overline{Q'GH}$  is collinear, or   $$ Q'+\overline{Q'}GH=G+H $$  Note  $P^2+x^2=1/2(\frac{x^2}{y}+\frac{x^2}{z}+2x+y+z-\frac{xy}{z}-\frac{xz}{y})P$   (as  $P$  is a root of the quadratic equation with  $I,J$  as roots)
Thus 
\begin{align*}
Q'+\overline{Q'}GH&=
x+2y+y^2x-\frac{yP}{x}-\frac{xy}{P}&=x+2y+y^2x-\frac{yP^2+x^2y}{xP}
&=x+2y+\frac{y^2}{x}-1/2(x+\frac{xy}{z}+2y+y^2x+\frac{yz}{x}-\frac{y^2}{z}-z)
&=1/2(\frac{y^2}{x}+\frac{y^2}{z}+2y+x+z-\frac{yx}{z}-\frac{yz}{x})
&=G+H
\end{align*}
Thus  $Q'$  lies on  $GH$  and  $Q'=Q$  which solves the question

